# Media Lifecycle Invariants — Read Before "Simplifying"

Each item below came out of a full-app audit that fixed a real bug. In every case the
*pre-fix* version looks like the simpler, more obvious code, so these are exactly the
lines a future cleanup pass tends to revert. Keep the behaviour unless the user asks
for a change and names the item.

## Warm web views are capped and destroyed, not just dropped

`WarmWebSessionPool` enforces `maxWebViews` and calls `WarmWebSession.destroy()` on
eviction. Each warm page is a real web content process; releasing the reference without
tearing down the `WKWebView`, its message handler, and its delegates leaks the process.

Launch does **not** warm websites. Show website cards warm via `warmSoon(_:)` when a
Show opens; bookmarks warm one at a time as they scroll into view, spaced and
watchdogged. Do not reintroduce a launch `warmAll()` that warms every bookmark up
front — that competed with the first frame and made the whole app sluggish at launch.

## Ambient audio pauses, it never stops

`AudioAmbientPolicy.applyYieldIfNeeded(for:)` / `forWebMedia:` call `pause()` (with fade)
when Settings → Pause Music for Video is on. `stop()` tears down the queue and mini
player, so yielding to audible video or website media would silently discard the user's
playback state. Pausing is the point, not an oversight. Default is off: music keeps
playing under video. Muted library video and silent web pages do not yield even when
the preference is on. Audible presentation uses `.moviePlayback` only when yielding;
otherwise it stays on `.default` so the mix is not ducked.

## Only phone-screen video registers for focus suspension

`VideoFocusCoordinator` pauses registered video on `willResignActive` and resumes it on
`didBecomeActive`, so minimising Eclipse (or peeking at the app switcher) does not leave
a clip playing audio behind the app. Registration is deliberately narrow: the hero
Practice Mode player and the fullscreen phone previews. Registering the AirPlay path
(`PresentationViewController.player`, its screensaver loop, or the web views) would undo
`ExternalDisplayManager.keepPresentationAlive()` and blank the TV the moment the operator
opens another app; ambient music owns the `audio` background mode and must keep playing.
Phone web media is out for a second reason: the browser's `EclipseWebMediaSync` reporter
forwards its pause to the TV.

Only videos the coordinator itself paused are resumed, so a video the user paused by hand
stays paused, and the second lifecycle notification of one trip out of the app cannot
widen that set. A player in Picture in Picture reports itself as not playing rather than
suspending: PiP *is* the user asking for playback outside the app, which is why
`PlayerFocusSuspension` takes over the `AVPlayerViewController` delegate.

## Photo capture has no fallback

`capturePhotoToLibrary` uses only the full-resolution frame it renders on demand and
reports `CaptureError.noFrame` otherwise. Do not "improve" it by falling back to
`latestSampleImage` or `lastFrame`: both are downscaled to `tileStillMaxEdge`, and
`lastFrame` is persisted and can outlive the session that produced it, so a fallback
writes a stale thumbnail into the user's photo library instead of failing honestly.

Related: `latestSampleImage` must keep exactly one writer (the throttled tile path) so
it stays tile-sized. A second writer storing full-resolution frames is what made the
above bug possible.

## Grid layout floors its tile dimensions

`LibraryGridViewController+HomeLayout` routes tile sizing through `columnWidth(...)`,
which floors the result at `minimumTileSide`. `NSCollectionLayoutDimension.absolute`
throws on a non-positive value, and the container width is briefly zero during early
layout, so the `max(...)` is a crash guard rather than defensive noise.

## Sync only reports success it can back up

`iPhoneConnectionManager.replayLibrary` counts per-item send failures and calls
`completion(false)` unless every item transferred. Reporting unconditional success made
`MultiTVSyncCoordinator` mark a replica caught up when it wasn't, which suppressed the
retry. Similarly, `pendingRestoreId` clears only *after* a successful send, and
cancelling a transfer must also cancel the replica fan-out.

## Unreadable saved pages are preserved, not overwritten

`WebPageStore.load()` salvages entries individually and, when it still can't decode,
copies the raw bytes to a backup key and starts empty. It must never write over data it
failed to read — that turns a decode bug into permanent loss of the user's bookmarks.

## tvOS: one teardown path for the video player

Stopping playback goes through `ImageViewController.retireCurrentPlayer(...)`. Ad-hoc
teardown is how videos ended up still playing audio after a transition to an image or
the grid, with companions left showing a stale playing state.

## tvOS: granular data-source updates do not also reload

`MediaDataSource`'s add/remove/move notify only their specific delegate callback. They
must not also call `mediaDataDidChange()`; pairing a batch update with a full
`reloadData()` stomps the animation and can crash. Selection is re-validated in the
batch-update completion instead.

## Synced media is never re-enqueued to carry preferences

`CaptureStore.idsNeedingUpload` returns `.pendingUpload` rows only. Including `.synced`
rows looks like the cheap way to propagate Fit mode, framing, loop, and mute, since
those live on the MediaItem record but are written by plain settings helpers. It is
not: `makeMediaRecord` stamps a fresh `modifiedAt`, so every foreground rewrote every
capture record, and every *other* device then re-fetched those records — asset bytes
included — and discarded them. It also silently clobbered preferences, because
`applyRemoteMediaPrefs` has no clock, so an idle phone foregrounding overwrote edits
made elsewhere. Preference edits travel through `CloudKitMediaDirtyStore`, flagged by
`scheduleMediaPrefsSave(libraryId:)`, which deliberately leaves `syncState` alone so
the asset is not re-attached.

## Fetched MediaItem assets are kept, and evictions are remembered

`CKSyncEngine` fetches records with their assets and has no field-level `desiredKeys`
equivalent — `nextFetchChangesOptions` narrows by zone only, and assets cannot move to
an excluded zone because a `CKShare` root and its children must share one. The bytes
have already crossed the network when a record arrives, and CloudKit purges its staging
copy, so `applyRemoteMedia` copies them into `LocalMediaStore` instead of discarding
them and paying for a second download on tap.

`CloudKitEvictedMediaStore` is what keeps that honest. `.remoteOnly` means both "never
had it" and "user reclaimed the space", so without a separate eviction index any later
edit to an item, on any device, would restore a download the user just deleted.

## Every `CKContainer` comes from `CloudKitAvailability`

`CKContainer(identifier:)` *traps* rather than failing when the identifier is missing
from the app's entitlements. CI builds with `CODE_SIGNING_ALLOWED=NO`, so the XCTest
host runs unentitled and a single unguarded container takes the process down at launch —
before one test can report. The environment check in `CloudKitAvailability` looks like
test-awareness leaking into production code, which is why it keeps getting deleted; it
is the only thing keeping the suite runnable.

So `CloudKitSyncEngine.init` takes a required container with no default argument, and
`ShowLiveSession.refreshCloudKitUser` leaves `userHash` nil instead of trapping. A
default argument that builds a container inline is the trap wearing a disguise: it
moves the crash to whichever caller omits the parameter. Callers degrade — sync reports
itself paused via `DisabledSyncBackend`, and a nil `userHash` already disables director
advertising, operator browsing, and invitations.

## One projector signal drives affordances, another drives hardware

`LiveOutputRouting.projectorAvailable` reads `ExternalDisplayManager.isAirPlayAvailable`,
which is deliberately wider than `isConnected`: on iOS 27+ the scene accessory reports a
display before its scene attaches, and `present(_:)` attaches on demand. Every badge,
gate, and "what does this tap mean" decision has to read the same one. Mixing them made
the app disagree with itself — the Show marked taps live and let websites go live off
`isAirPlayAvailable` while Live Poll refused the same deck with "Live Poll needs AirPlay,
HDMI, or Practice Mode."

Side effects that drive real hardware are the exception and keep reading `isConnected`:
parking EclipseTV (`AirPlayOverlayPark`), director election in `ShowLiveSession`, and
camera mirroring. An unattached scene cannot render, so parking the TV for one just
blanks the room.

---

If one of these genuinely needs to change, STOP and ask the user first, naming the item.
